You need a server endpoint — Node/Express, Next.js API route, serverless function, anything. Install the Stripe SDK server-side:

```bash npm install stripe ```

```js import Stripe from 'stripe'; const stripe = new Stripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);

app.post('/api/create-payment-intent', async (req, res) => { const { amount } = req.body; // in cents: $20.00 -> 2000

// IMPORTANT: never trust the amount from the client alone. // Look up the price from your database or price catalog. const paymentIntent = await stripe.paymentIntents.create({ amount: validatedAmount, currency: 'usd', automatic_payment_methods: { enabled: true }, });

res.json({ clientSecret: paymentIntent.client_secret }); }); ```

The comment about validating the amount is the single most important line here. A client can send any number it wants; your server must determine the price from its own data. Every "I got charged $0.01 for a $500 product" story starts with trusting client input.

Use restricted API keys and keep the secret key in environment variables, never in the repo.

Install the React bindings:

```bash npm install @stripe/stripe-js @stripe/react-stripe-js ```

```tsx import { loadStripe } from '@stripe/stripe-js'; import { Elements, PaymentElement, useStripe, useElements } from '@stripe/react-stripe-js';

const stripePromise = loadStripe(import.meta.env.VITE_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY);

function CheckoutPage() { const [clientSecret, setClientSecret] = useState('');

useEffect(() => { fetch('/api/create-payment-intent', { method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, body: JSON.stringify({ productId: 'pro-plan' }), }) .then(r => r.json()) .then(d => setClientSecret(d.clientSecret)); }, []);

if (!clientSecret) return <p>Loading checkout…</p>;

return ( <Elements stripe={stripePromise} options={{ clientSecret }}> <CheckoutForm /> </Elements> ); }

function CheckoutForm() { const stripe = useStripe(); const elements = useElements();

const handleSubmit = async (e) => { e.preventDefault(); if (!stripe || !elements) return;

const { error } = await stripe.confirmPayment({ elements, confirmParams: { return_url: 'https://your-site.com/success' }, }); if (error) showErrorToCustomer(error.message); };

return ( <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}> <PaymentElement /> <button disabled={!stripe}>Pay</button> </form> ); } ```

The `PaymentElement` is the right choice for most integrations: it automatically shows cards, Apple Pay, Google Pay, and local payment methods based on the customer's location, and it stays updated as Stripe adds methods. Building custom card inputs with `CardElement` is more work for no benefit unless your design demands it.

The publishable key is safe in client code — it's designed to be public. The secret key never leaves the server.
